  <h1>Signal Confidence in the Officer's Hand</h1>
  <p class="lede">Where a live tracking-status icon belongs in a field app's top bar — and why putting the system's own confidence in front of the person carrying the device is the cheapest feedback loop in the whole operation.</p>

  <div class="redacted">
    <div>
      <span class="label">No real data</span>
      <p>Client names, site addresses and numbers, shift identifiers, personnel initials, repository and file paths, internal component names and the assistant's product name have all been replaced with neutral placeholders. The reasoning, the constraints and the recommendation are unchanged. See the substitution table at the end.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<section>
  <p class="kicker">The main idea</p>
  <h2>The icon is a false-positive reducer, not chrome</h2>
  <p>A field officer's phone continuously reports its position while on shift. That signal carries a <strong>confidence</strong> — sometimes the fix is good, sometimes the satellite geometry has collapsed and the position is a guess, sometimes the app has stopped reporting altogether.</p>
  <p>Today the officer cannot see any of that. The confidence is visible only downstream, to the operations centre and to the automation that reads the feed. So when the signal degrades, the officer — the one person who could fix it in five seconds by reopening the app — is the only participant who does not know.</p>

  <div class="thesis">
    <p>An officer who can <strong>see</strong> that the signal has gone uncertain can restart the app before the operations centre ever receives an event.</p>
    <cite>The argument the whole placement study exists to serve</cite>
  </div>

  <p>That reframes the work. This is not a request to add an icon to a toolbar; it is a proposal to close a control loop at its cheapest point. The placement question only matters because the icon has to be <em>glanceable on every screen, in a fixed position, without a label</em> for that loop to close.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="kicker">The flow</p>
  <h2>Two paths for the same degraded signal</h2>
  <p>This is the connection between the patrol in the field and the automation that watches it. The same event — accuracy collapsing mid-patrol — travels one of two routes depending on whether anyone in the field can see it.</p>

