    <figcaption><b>The automation is not wrong on the upper path — it is working correctly on a signal that lied to it.</b> An agent that receives a degraded position with no way to know it is degraded must treat it as real, so it escalates, and every handoff after that is expensive and unnecessary. The lower path does not make the agents smarter; it removes the input that fooled them. That is the general lesson worth carrying: <em>before adding intelligence downstream, check whether someone upstream could have seen the problem and simply been told.</em></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="note">
    <h4>Why the vocabulary must match the control room</h4>
    <p>The icon's four states deliberately reuse the same colour semantics the operations map already applies to incoming position pings. An officer saying "mine is amber" and a controller seeing amber are then describing the same thing — which is what makes a phone call short instead of diagnostic.</p>
  </div>
