      <p>Reporting, accuracy within tolerance. Nothing to do.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="sw"><i style="background:var(--uncertain)"></i>Low accuracy</span>
      <p><strong>The state that justifies the whole feature.</strong> Still reporting, but the position cannot be trusted. Actionable by the officer alone.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="sw"><i style="background:var(--none)"></i>Not tracking</span>
      <p>Should be reporting and is not — permission revoked, service killed, or the app was force-closed.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <span class="sw"><i style="background:var(--off)"></i>Off / no shift</span>
      <p>Correctly inactive. Rendered grey rather than removed, so the position is learned.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note">
    <h4>Absence renders as a state, not as nothing</h4>
    <p>The icon is greyed when off-shift rather than hidden. Two reasons: a control that appears and disappears is never learned, and — more importantly — "off" and "broken" must look different. A missing icon would collapse those two into one.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <p class="kicker">The method</p>
  <h2>Argue placement from the component contract, not from taste</h2>
  <p>The study is built as a faithful interactive mock of the real home screen on two device profiles, with every icon the top bar can currently display and under what condition. Placement is then argued from what the shared bar component <em>already guarantees</em>:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>The notifications bell is appended <strong>after</strong> the caller-supplied action list, so it is always rightmost on every screen that uses the shared bar. It is the app's fixed anchor.</li>
    <li>Therefore inserting the new icon into that action list lands it <strong>immediately left of the bell</strong>, on every screen and every device profile, as a one-line change — inheriting the anchor rather than competing with it.</li>
    <li>The leading area is a single tap target that opens the account screen, and its contents can be hidden by profile configuration. Anything anchored there can be stranded.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="opts">
    <div class="opt">
      <span class="id">A</span>
      <div>
        <h4>Right cluster, between the avatar and the bell<span class="verdict v-yes">recommended</span></h4>
        <p>Inherits the app-wide anchor, needs no new layout, appears identically on both device profiles, and is glanceable on every screen. One-line insertion into the existing action list.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="opt">
      <span class="id">B</span>
      <div>
        <h4>Left, beside the rating indicator<span class="verdict v-no">rejected</span></h4>
        <p>The leading area reads as "my status", which fits semantically — but the whole area is one tap target to the account screen today, so adding a second action there invites mis-taps. And the rating can be hidden by configuration, which would leave the icon stranded beside nothing.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="opt">
      <span class="id">C</span>
      <div>
        <h4>Status chip inside the shift card<span class="verdict v-mb">companion, not replacement</span></h4>
        <p>Has room for a text label, which the top-bar icon does not. But it scrolls away with content and is absent from every other screen — so it cannot carry the glanceability requirement. Best shipped <em>alongside</em> A, where the label teaches the icon's meaning.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Constraints found in the existing UI</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>Roughly three actions is the practical ceiling for a top bar under standard platform guidance — adding a fourth crowds the title, which on one device profile is already long.</li>
    <li>Minimum 48&nbsp;dp touch target, preserved at the icon's small visual size.</li>
    <li class="no">Do not remove the icon when inactive. Grey it, so its position is always the same place.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

